    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Learning About Quadratic Equations and Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00063">Watch the following video to learn more about quadratic equations and
        graphs.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://openstax.org/r/quadratic-equations"/>
      </media>
    </section>

    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00064"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_7_16_3_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Matching Graphs and Equations</title>
        <para id="para-00066">When matching graphs of quadratic functions and their equations, look
          for the matching vertex. Then look if the parabola is opening up or down. Finally, use
          another point to determine if the graph has been compressed or stretched. Recall this type
          of transformation will affect the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-value of the equation.</para>
        <para id="para-00067">Looking at the graph below, choose the equation that matches the
          graph.</para>
        <para id="para-00068">
          <media alt="Graph of a parabola on a coordinate plane. The x-axis has a scale of 1 extending from negative 10.5 to 10.5. The vertical-axis extends from approximately negative 10.5 to approximately 10.5 with a scale of 1.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_18_08_01.jpg" width="300"/>
          </media>

        </para>
        <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" number-style="lower-alpha" id="list-00002">
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00069"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the vertex.</para><para id="para-00065">The vertex is at <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00070"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Does the parabola open up or down?</para><para id="para-00094">The
          parabola opens up. So the leading coefficient is positive.</para>
        <para id="para-00071">The correct answer is b. <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00072">Remember the vertex form is <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mi>h</m:mi>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mi>k</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. If the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-variable is being added in the
          parenthesis, the parabola has been shifted left.</para><para id="para-00095">The <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> moves the
          parabola left 2.</para><para id="para-00096">The -3 shifts it down 3 from the origin.</para>
        <para id="para-00073"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Use the vertex form of the equation and
          write the parts you know: the coordinates of the vertex. <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00074"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - To solve for <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, find another point on the
          graph of the parabola and plug in those values for <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> in the equation you wrote.</para><para id="para-00097">
          Substitute the point <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> into the
          equation to check the value of <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. We have hypothesized <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, and with the coordinates from
          the second point, <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00075"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Solve for <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para><para id="para-00098"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. Now solve for <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> to find that <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00076"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Plug the value for <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> back into the equation.</para><para id="para-00099">So, <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Matching Graphs and Equations</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
            <problem id="prob-00007">
              <para id="para-00077">Match the graph with its equation in vertex form.</para>
              <para id="para-00078">
                <media alt="Graph of a parabola on a coordinate plane. The x-axis has a scale of 1 extending from negative 10.5 to 10.5. The vertical-axis extends from negative 10 to 10 with a scale of 1.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_18_08_02.jpg" width="300"/>
                </media>

              </para>
              <para id="para-00079">a. <m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00080">b. <m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00081">c. <m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00082">d. <m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00083"/>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00007">
              <para id="para-00084">Here is how to match a graph and its equation:</para>
              <para id="para-00085"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the vertex. <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00086"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Does the parabola open up or down?
          Up — this means the leading coefficient is positive.</para>
              <para id="para-00087"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Use the vertex form of the
          equation and write the parts you know: the coordinates of the vertex. <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00088"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - To solve for <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>, find another
          point on the graph of the parabola and plug in those values for <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> in the
          equation you wrote. <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          is also on the graph of this parabola. So, <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>.</para>
              <para id="para-00089"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Solve for <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>.
                <newline/> <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00090"><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00091"><m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00093"><m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00092"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Plug the value for <newline/><m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> back into the
          equation. <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math><newline/>
    <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> </para>
                <para id="para-00100">d.</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
  </section>
  </content>
</document>